ation current density (which depends on $S$ through the effective recombination at both interfaces, e.g. $J_0 \propto S$ in the surface-recombination-limited regime), $n$ is the ideality factor, $T$ the temperature, $R_{sh}$ the shunt resistance, and $q,k_B$ have their usual meanings. The open-circuit voltage is $V_{oc} = V$ such that $J(V)=0$, and the maximum-power-point voltage $V_{mpp}$ maximizes $V\cdot J(V)$. In addition, the spatial distribution of the built-in electric field across the absorber and the transport layers — determined by the dielectric permittivities, doping densities, and band offsets of ETL/HTL — modulates the effective collection efficiency $\eta_c \in (0,1]$ multiplying $J_{\text{ph}}$, with most of the potential dropping over the transport layers when those layers are weakly doped or thick.\n\nFormulate and solve the following optimization problem: choose the transport-layer parameters (thickness, doping density, dielectric constant, band offset, and resulting interface surface recombination velocity $S$) so as to maximize the power conversion efficiency\n$$ \eta \;=\; \frac{\max_V \, V\,J(V)}{P_{\text{in}}}, $$\nsubject to the trade-off that decreasing $S$ (which raises $V_{oc}$) generally increases $R_s$ and may redistribute the electric field in a way that reduces $\eta_c$ at $V_{mpp}$. Specifically: (i) derive analytic conditions characterizing the optimal $(S, R_s, \eta_c)$ triple for which $V_{mpp}\cdot J(V_{mpp})$ is maximized, and (ii) determine quantitatively how the resistive and field-distribution losses at the ETL/HTL interfaces should be balanced against the recombination losses in order to achieve the highest attainable efficiency.","status_search_result":"The interplay between interface recombination velocity, series resistance, and electric-field distribution in perovskite solar cells remains an active topic in device physics. While many partial models (drift-diffusion simulations, equivalent-circuit analyses) have been developed, no closed-form joint optimization of $S$, $R_s$, and field-distribution losses for ETL/HTL design has been broadly accepted, and reviews continue to list it as an open device-engineering problem.","status_evidence":[{"title":"High Open-Circuit Voltages in Lead-Halide Perovskite Solar Cells","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.08752","snippet":"we need a better understanding of resistive and charge collection losses at interfaces and in electron and hole transport layers.","claim":"Authors explicitly identify the trade-off between low S, low R_s, and favorable field distribution as an open question."}]},"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"96be11f2-f97a-46ee-bfa3-d5a2fa85eded"}
